Pediculosis Capitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Live lice, Adherent nits, Scalp pruritus

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-035
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, severe scalp itching leading to sleeplessness and distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Pediatric patient, primary caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a young child experiencing intense, unrelenting scalp pruritus causing severe sleep deprivation, psychological distress, and extensive excoriation of the scalp and neck. The caregiver reports a persistent "crawling" sensation and extreme anxiety regarding a potentially highly contagious infestation affecting the entire household. Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform a definitive physical examination of the scalp to accurately diagnose or exclude Pediculosis Capitis versus severe Seborrheic Dermatitis, rapidly institute effective pediculicidal therapy to break the transmission cycle, and manage the acute pruritus preventing sleep. Delaying assessment poses an unacceptable risk of prolonged suffering, secondary bacterial infection from severe scratching, and unchecked household spread, fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: She hasn't slept in two nights because her head is intensely itchy, and I keep feeling a tickling movement in her hair.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated approximately 2 weeks ago with mild scratching, which has acutely escalated over the past 48 hours into intense, severe scalp pruritus. The child describes a persistent "tickling sensation of movement" in the hair. The pruritus is reported as significantly worse at night, preventing any restorative sleep. The caregiver notes the child is constantly scratching, particularly focusing on the retroauricular (behind the ears) and occipital regions. The caregiver has noted numerous excoriations on the posterior neck. Denies fever, generalized rash, or focal neurological deficits.
Functional Status: Pediatric patient. Extremely fatigued and irritable due to profound sleep disturbance.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with siblings. Extremely high risk of immediate household and school transmission. High caregiver anxiety.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 100/65 mmHg (approx) | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 22 Kg
General: Alert, but visibly exhausted and constantly scratching the back of the head.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Microscopic examination of a plucked hair showing an intact louse or viable nit would assist in confirming the diagnosis in borderline cases, but direct visualization is sufficient for acute management.
Physical Exam: Thorough examination of the scalp, particularly the occipital and retroauricular regions, reveals definitive visual visualization of live, crawling lice on the scalp surface. Numerous adherent, grayish-white nits (eggs) are firmly attached to the hair shafts, clustered within 1cm of the scalp base. Extensive excoriations are visible across the scalp and extending down the posterior neck, with mild erythema but no frank honey-colored crusting (no severe secondary bacterial impetiginization currently). Occasional, mildly tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy is palpable. The remainder of the skin is clear.
Environmental Observations: Shared bedroom with siblings; soft toys and shared bedding are present.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Caregiver applied an over-the-counter anti-dandruff shampoo with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Pediculosis Capitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Seborrheic Dermatitis (Dandruff) - Ruled out definitively as the white specks (nits) are firmly adherent to the hair shaft and cannot be easily flicked off, and live crawling insects were directly visualized.
Tinea Capitis (Ringworm) - Unlikely as there are no distinct, circular patches of alopecia (hair loss) with "black dot" broken hairs or severe, boggy inflammatory masses (kerion).
Atopic Dermatitis - Ruled out as the primary cause given the direct visualization of the parasite, though underlying atopy can exacerbate the hypersensitivity reaction to the louse saliva.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (High risk of rapid household spread)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of severe, unrelenting scalp pruritus causing sleep deprivation and extensive excoriation necessitated an urgent clinical assessment. By performing a direct visual examination in the home, the diagnosis of active Pediculosis Capitis was definitively established, successfully ruling out severe inflammatory dermatoses. Immediate intervention with potent topical pediculicides and sedating antihistamines was mandated to halt the infestation, stop the acute suffering, and allow the child to sleep. Furthermore, initiating strict, immediate household decontamination protocols was critical to prevent further transmission. This comprehensive acute intervention could not safely wait until the following day, firmly satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a targeted topical pediculicide (e.g., Permethrin 1% lotion or Malathion 0.5% liquid), to be applied strictly according to manufacturer instructions. Advised a sedating antihistamine (e.g., Promethazine) for 2-3 nights to suppress the intense histaminergic pruritus and facilitate sleep while the bites heal.
Positive Behavioral Change: To permanently break the life cycle of the lice, you must mechanically physically remove the unhatched eggs (nits) using a specialized fine-toothed metal comb coated in thick white hair conditioner every 2 days for the next 10 days. The chemical treatment kills the live bugs, but the thick conditioner physically suffocates and traps the newly hatching babies before they can mate, ensuring the infestation is entirely eradicated.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Fine-toothed metal lice comb required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner or School Nurse in 7 days, or sooner if thick, yellow crusts develop on the scratched areas (indicating bacterial infection).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department or Urgent Care if the scratched areas on the scalp or neck become swollen, hot, bright red, or begin leaking thick yellow pus, or if the child develops a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is confirmed visually. Re-treatment in 7-10 days is almost always necessary to kill lice that hatch after the initial treatment.
Clinical Course: The live lice will die within hours of the chemical application. However, the intense itching will persist for several days as the skin continues to react to the louse saliva already injected into the scalp.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if the lymph nodes in the neck become massively swollen, red, and painful to the touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The itching is an allergic reaction to the saliva of the lice. The prescribed lotion is a targeted insecticide that rapidly paralyzes and kills the live bugs.
Actionable Steps: Immediately wash all bedding, towels, and clothing worn in the past 48 hours in hot water and dry them on the highest heat setting. Any items that cannot be washed (like stuffed animals) must be sealed tightly in a plastic bag for 2 weeks to starve any remaining lice.

